## Daily bid briefing — SPP, bid date 2026-08-05

**Run:** clean, exit 0. Log at `QCTest/Manual_bidding/logs/bid_analysis_20260804.log`. Two environment notes: a stray `/tmp/inspect.py` (another user's file) shadows the stdlib `inspect` module, so the script must not live in or run from `/tmp` — I ran it from `logs/scratch/`. Today's log had been left 0 bytes by an earlier attempt that hit exactly that.

### 1. Bid date
`bid_dt=2026-08-05`, `dt=2026-08-04` (pre-10am CT, so one day out). **11 constraints** cleared the 500 threshold: 55 DA vs 20 RT constraint-rows. DA for `dt` has cleared; RT for `dt` is only ~7 hours settled, so all RT figures below come from days strictly before `dt`.

### 2. Zonal setup — and a correction that drives everything
The `tomorrow_pct` summary reads mild, and that reading is misleading:

| zone | ow | fw | ol | fl |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 6 | 12 | 21 | 50 |
| 2 | 61 | 46 | 29 | 58 |
| **3** | **90** | 80 | **90** | 83 |
| 4 | 61 | 79 | 56 | 67 |
| 5 | 41 | 11 | 16 | 38 |
| 21 | 9 | 22 | 66 | 51 |

Flagged: **zone 3 high on all four** (ow 90 / ol 90); **zone 1 and 21 bottom-decile wind** (ow 6, ow 9); zone 5 low load (ol 16).

The trap: those percentiles are vs **the trailing month at the same hour**, but `analyze_constraint_by_zone` conditions on the **top 5% of the trailing 2 years, all hours**. Different yardsticks. Measured against the 2-year q95 the lift actually uses:

- **Zone 4 load peaks at 32,703 MW vs a q95 of 29,521 — 111% of threshold, 8 hours above it (HE13–HE20).** So a "56th percentile" load day is squarely inside the top-5% window. Zone 3 load: 115%, 12 hours (HE12–HE23).
- **Wind reaches nothing.** Zone 4 wind tops out at 76% of q95; zone 3 wind clears it for a single hour (HE24). Zones 1/5/21 are at 21/45/50%.

**Tomorrow is a high-load, no-wind day.** Load-driven constraints get their condition; wind-driven ones do not. My first read of the mild percentiles had this backwards.

I also checked whether the big thin-record lifts were an artifact — they are not. `ln6_me4-sw51` put 8 of its 9 lifetime binding hours inside the top-5% zone-4 load window; `lnbois_tap` 42 of 44; `lnmdwst-frnkln1` 44 of 49. Real concentration, small samples. Separately, **zone 21 has forecast data for only 3,048 of 17,520 hours (17%)**, so its ~161-hour conditioning window is drawn from a different period — ignore zone-21 lifts when comparing zones.

